AD  - Moscow Technical University of Communications and Informatics, Moscow, Russian Federation
AB  - This article addresses the problem of evaluating energy efficiency and data throughput in decentralized LoRa mesh networks under dynamically changing propagation conditions. In contrast to centralized LoRaWAN architectures, where devices communicate exclusively through gateways, the mesh topology considered here enables multi-hop data delivery via intermediate nodes. This is especially relevant in conditions of urban dense development, where direct node-to-gateway links are often disrupted by obstacles or excessive distance. A simulation model of the physical layer (PHY) of LoRa is developed in MATLAB, incorporating an AWGN channel and a frequency-selective Rayleigh TDL channel calibrated according to ITU-R P.1411-10 recommendations. The packet error rate (PER) and bit error rate (BER) are used as the primary quality metrics, while network throughput and end-to-end reliability are evaluated as functions of the number of relay hops. The effect of the LoRa coding rate (CR = 4/5 versus CR = 4/8) on the residual error floor and end-to-end performance is investigated as a means of mitigating irreducible fading losses. ©2026 IEEE.
