AD  - Istanbul Sabahattin Zaim University, Department Of Software Engineering, Istanbul, Turkey
AD  - Istanbul Sabahattin Zaim University, Department Of Computer Engineering, Istanbul, Turkey
AB  - This paper presents a performance comparison of three LoRa-based communication systems. Mesh Networks, Point-to-Point (P2P), and satellite gateways for Lunar-IoT networks. In this study, an evaluation framework has been developed that reflects the lunar environment. This includes factors like radiation-induced bit errors, extreme temperature changes (+130°C to -170°C), 14-day day/night cycles, terrain-related path loss from craters and regolith, and vacuum propagation. In testing 25 distributed nodes over a 5 km coverage area, the mesh network achieved a packet delivery rate (PDR) of 97.06%. In contrast, the P2P architecture had a PDR of 23.60%, while the satellite system achieved 74.15%. P2P had the lowest latency at (319 ms), and the least energy use, but single-hop limits resulted in 76% of transmissions failing in low-density lunar deployments. Satellite communication ensured reliable connections but came with much higher latency (2079 ms) and energy usage. Networks using multi-hop routing with a 1.5 km distance between nodes created the best balance of reliability and efficiency for lunar operations. This study provides performance comparisons essential for planning a LoRa-based communication system on the Moon. © 2026 IEEE.
