AD  - Velammal College of Engineering and Technology, Department of Electronics and Communication Engineering, Madurai, India
AB  - This research addresses the critical failure of conventional communication infrastructures during climatic disasters, particularly in geographically isolated regions where "last mile"connectivity is often the first to fail. Conventional systems like GSM and standard LoRaWAN suffer from single points of failure and congestion. To overcome these limitations, we propose a robust, autonomously survivable architecture synthesizing Multi-Hop Low energy Adaptive Clustering Hierarchy (LEACH) protocols with the Long Range (LoRa) physical layer. The proposed method introduces a decentralized mesh topology controlled by a novel "Node Rank"(NR) algorithmic fitness function, which deterministically assigns Cluster Heads based on residual energy, proximity to the sink, and connectivity degree. Extensive stochastic modeling and discrete-event simulations in Network Simulator 2 (NS2) demonstrate that this approach achieves a 96% Packet Delivery Ratio (PDR), reduces end-to-end latency to 1.9s, and extends network lifetime by 80% compared to AODV and single-hop paradigms. These results confirm the system's viability as a resilient backbone for next-generation disaster informatics, ensuring reliable early warnings even when traditional networks collapse. © 2026 IEEE.
