AB  - The need to offer reliable low-cost backhaul connectivity in rural areas is a decisive issue to large scale Internet of Things (IoT) implementations, where standard wireless network solutions are characterized by high latency, subpar coverage, and limited scalability. This is a work that proposes a Hybrid LoRa-Visible-Light Communication (VLC) Mesh architecture that intends on providing IoT backhaul with ultra-low-latency and low-energy usage and low-cost backhaul in underserved regions. The aim of the suggested design is to integrate long-range, low-power robustness of LoRa with high-speed, short-range of VLC in a mesh topology, hence providing strong coverage and high throughput data transmission. The originality of the given approach is that it combines LoRa and VLC technologies seamlessly into a cooperative mesh structure, which allows multi-hop adaptive routing with the minimal transmission delays and maximized energy use. According to experimental assessments and simulations, the backhaul solutions with multiple technologies show a significant reduction in latency, throughput performance, and energy efficiency over the traditional single-technology solutions. This hybrid system presents a sustainable and scalable way of achieving the realization of smart villages, accuracy agriculture, and disaster-resilient IoT systems in rural areas.  © 2026 IEEE.
