AD  - Graduate School of Computer Science and Systems Engineering, Kyushu Institute of Technology, Fukuoka, Iizuka-shi, Japan
AD  - Faculty of Computer Science and Systems Engineering, Kyushu Institute of Technology, Fukuoka, Iizuka-shi, Japan
AB  - LoRaWAN is widely used in smart agriculture and smart cities for its low power consumption, long-range communication, and cost efficiency. However, its effectiveness declines in environments with dense buildings or forests, where obstacles impede communication. To address this, we propose and evaluate a LoRaWAN-based Mesh (LoRaMesh) Network. We compare the maximum communication distance achievable in a one-hop scenario with obstacles to that in a two-hop scenario using a relay node. Our system uses three Arduino units with LoRa Shields, configured as transmitter, relay, and receiver nodes, respectively, to form a mesh topology. Experiments are conducted in two environments: one with a clear line of sight and another with dense obstacles. In the clear environment, the relay increases the maximum communication distance by 1.5 km, while in the obstacle-rich one, the increase is 420 m-about one-third of that in the clear environment. These results show that mesh networking can effectively extend LoRaWAN coverage even in an obstructed environment, but the degree of improvement is highly dependent on the environment surrounding the receiver nodes. © 2025 IEEE.
