IS  - 2025
DO  - 10.1109/WF-IoT64238.2025.11270542
UR  - https://www.scopus.com/pages/publications/105032644534?origin=resultslist
AD  - Technical University of Denmark, Department of Electrical and Photonics Engineering, Kgs. Lyngby, Denmark
AB  - Environmental monitoring in the Arctic presents extreme challenges due to its vast, infrastructure-sparse terrain, harsh climate, and strict environmental regulations. Traditional communication methods such as satellite links or manual data retrieval are power-hungry, costly, or operationally impractical. In response, we introduce ArcLoRaM, a custom mesh protocol for ultra-low-power, long-range data transmission using sub-Ghz LoRa technology. ArcLoRaM is designed specifically for remote deployments where node autonomy and longevity are critical. It combines a hybrid mesh-of-stars topology with a time-synchronized TDMA communication scheme, decentralized power-aware routing, and self-healing capabilities. The protocol dynamically balances network load based on battery levels and hop distances, ensuring long-term operation while avoiding communication bottlenecks. We validate ArcLoRaM through simulations that analyze data throughput, collision probability, and energy efficiency under realistic regulatory constraints. Benchmark scenarios demonstrate the protocol's ability to scale from compact clusters to wide-area deployments exceeding 30,000 square kilometers, as well as its suitability for low-data-rate applications with minimal infrastructure. These results confirm that ArcLoRaM enables reliable and sustainable data collection in Arctic and similarly challenging environments. © 2025 IEEE.
