AB  - To address short battery lifetime, high energy consumption, collision-prone network construction, and limited abnormal-event responsiveness in large-scale LoRa-Mesh networks for fine particulate matter (PM2.5) monitoring, this study proposes a Hierarchical Adaptive Energy-Efficient (HAEE) protocol. HAEE jointly integrates Wake-on-Air (WoA)-assisted hierarchical construction, Data-Driven Adaptive Sampling (DDAS), and hierarchical abnormal-data forwarding. WoA assigns nodes to different layers according to their hop distance from the gateway and avoids blind network-wide flooding during initialization. DDAS dynamically adjusts the wake-up cycles of neighboring nodes according to PM2.5 thresholds and a topology-derived normalized Local Spatial Correlation (LSC) control index, reducing redundant sensing and communication under stable conditions. Abnormal data are forwarded to the gateway through Received Signal Strength Indicator (RSSI)-based upper-layer relay selection. Thirty-seed Monte Carlo simulations show that HAEE achieves shorter initialization time and lower packet collision rate than representative hierarchical and proactive LoRa-Mesh baselines. Under the unified state-based energy model, HAEE reduces network energy consumption over the 300-s evaluation window by approximately 84.7% at 300 nodes compared with the simultaneous wake-up strategy. The LSC-aware DDAS mechanism reduces simulated daily energy consumption per node from approximately 284 J/day to 78 J/day across the evaluated LSC range. In a synthetic spatio-temporal PM2.5 simulation, HAEE-DDAS achieves approximately 95.2% data fidelity and 99.9% abnormal-event recall. HAEE also improves network lifetime and delivery reliability under different LoRa physical-layer settings, traffic loads, channel conditions, and node-failure scenarios. These results establish the effectiveness of HAEE for long-term large-scale PM2.5 monitoring under the stated simulation configuration. © 2014 IEEE.
