AD  - Universiti Malaysia Sabah, Data Technology and Applications Research Group, Faculty of Computing and Informatics, Kota Kinabalu, Malaysia
AD  - Wuyi University, Faculty of Intelligent Manufacturing, Jiangmen, China
AB  - This study assesses a node-autonomous Adaptive Data Rate (ADR)-like algorithm's performance in optimizing Packet Delivery Ratio (PDR) in a LoRa multi-hop network, simulated in a forestry-like environment using Python with Visualize and matplotlib at 915 MHz. Using a log-distance path loss model, a three-node setup (transmitter, relay, gateway) spans 200-1000 meters, adjusting Spreading Factor (SF10-SF12), transmission power (14-20 dBm), and bandwidth (125-250 kHz). After 300 packets, PDR remains 100% up to 400 meters, drops to 40% at 500 meters, and nears 0% at 1,000 meters, with RSSI declining from -90 dBm to -125 dBm and SNR from 30 dB to 0 dB. Energy use rises from 0-2,000 mJ to over 8,000 mJ at 1000 meters due to retransmissions. At large scale After 1,000 packets, PDR fluctuates, dropping from 100% to 0% at 0-200 meters, peaking at 50% at 300 meters, 70% at 800 meters, and falling to 10% at 600 meters. Energy consumption spikes from 2,000 mJ to 30,000 mJ between 500-700 meters, dips to 5,000 mJ at 850 meters, and rises beyond 1,000 meters. Packet collisions increase from 30 to 50 at 550-600 meters, drop to 30 at 850 meters, and surge to 300 beyond 1,000 meters due to co-channel interference. The 500-meter limit suggests optimal relay placement. While effective at short ranges, the algorithm struggles at longer distances, indicating a need for coding rate adjustments or additional relays. This study offers insights for energy-efficient LoRaWAN deployments in challenging terrains, recommending real-world validation.  © 2025 IEEE.
