TI  - Node Failure Detection on a Hierarchy-Based Energy-Efficient Routing Protocol for LoRa-Mesh
PY  - 2026
T2  - 2026 IEEE International Black Seas Conference on Communications and Networking, BlackSeaCom 2026
DO  - 10.1109/BlackSeaCom69760.2026.11598298
UR  - https://www.scopus.com/pages/publications/105045601678?origin=resultslist
AD  - Halmstad University, School of Information Technology, Halmstad, Sweden
AD  - Federal University of Rio Grande do Sul, Porto Alegre, Brazil
AB  - Low-Power Wide-Area Networks (LPWANs) based on LoRa technology are widely used in Internet of Things (IoT) applications due to their long communication range and low energy consumption. While most LoRa deployments rely on star topologies, mesh networking protocols such as the Hierarchical Balanced Energy Efficient (HBEE) protocol enable multi-hop communication, extending coverage in challenging environments. However, node failures in LoRa mesh networks can significantly degrade performance when nodes continue forwarding packets through failed parents. This work proposes a lightweight failure detection mechanism for HBEE-based LoRa mesh networks. The approach uses passive overhearing to detect missing forwarding activity and applies timeout-based strategies to identify suspected node failures. When a failure is suspected, an on-demand ping message is used to confirm node availability and prevent further transmissions through inactive nodes. The proposed mechanism was evaluated experimentally using a LoRa mesh testbed. Results show that introducing failure detection improves network reliability under failure conditions, increasing the Packet Reception Rate from 79.17% to up to 86.67% without introducing significant additional energy consumption.  © 2026 IEEE.
