AB  - Maritime safety is a major concern for fishermen who operate near international borders, as unintentional crossings can lead to legal consequences and security risks. Present systems that utilise GSM-based tracking and LoRa WAN networks are significantly limited in deep-sea environments due to their low coverage and their dependence on centralised gateways. We propose a LoRa-based multi-hop mesh networking system that offers fishermen emergency distress signalling and real-time border notifications in order to overcome these difficulties. In order to combine power efficiency with prompt emergency response, the system incorporates GPS-based geofencing with a Point-in-Polygon (PIP) method, a 2-kilometer safety zone for early warning notifications, and an adaptive transmission mechanism. Our proposed LoRa mesh network uses multi-hop relaying to extend communication up to 140 kilometres in real-world marine environments, in contrast to standard LoRa WAN, which has a very restricted range. Experimental evaluations show that our approach greatly increases reliability and range, ensuring distress messages reach the coast. The proposed prototype model lowers socioeconomic risks, improves safety monitoring, and offers a long-lasting, reasonably priced marine security system by proactively avoiding unintentional crossings, thereby enhancing United Nations Sustainable Development Goals 9 and 11. © 2025 IEEE.
