AB  - Traditional weather monitoring infrastructure struggles to provide continuous, high-resolution data in remote rural areas and disaster zones where communication networks are sparse or overloaded. This paper presents a distributed drone-based weather observation system that uses LoRaWAN communication and an integrated LoRa mesh network to extend coverage and resilience. Each autonomous drone in the system is equipped with weather sensors (temperature, humidity, wind, air quality, GPS) and communicates over long-range, low-power LoRa links, enabling wide-area data collection independent of cellular networks. A key contribution is a custom peer-to-peer mesh networking algorithm that allows drones to relay and aggregate sensor data among themselves, so that only one drone needs a direct LoRaWAN gateway connection for the entire fleet. This approach eliminates single points of failure and balances energy use across drones, ensuring the network remains operational even if individual units go offline. We implemented a prototype with off-the-shelf hardware and demonstrated end-to-end connectivity through a LoRaWAN gateway to a cloud database, confirming reliable real-time data delivery over several kilometers. The proposed system is cost-effective, rapidly deploy-able, and scalable, making it well-suited for social applications such as rural agriculture monitoring, wildfire and flood response, and any scenario where timely local weather information is critical but conventional infrastructure is unavailable. ©2025 IEEE.
