AB  - Long Range Wide Area Network (LoRaWAN) is one of the most common broad-band low-power networks that use Semtech's patented LoRa modulation to provide high sensitivity for long-band rates at low data rates. LoRaWAN covers thousands of end-devices (sensors) that operate on batteries with single-hop gates. To communicate with the network server, every gateway needs an internet connection or a private network (Ethernet, WiFi, 3G/4G). Several gateways are needed to cover the final devices in obstructed areas with mountains, trees or simply vast areas. There could be some places where Internet/mobile coverage is lacking. This quickly generates Internet access costs in these areas unless a solution that routes messages between the gates through the current LoRa radio communications standard has been established. This reduces the internet connections available while providing coverage. In order that LoRaWAN devices with two-way capabilities should be supported according to regional regulations and that a road protocol should be provided that minimizes extra data that is provided with messages routing over a network. This solution was developed and evaluated. Triple gateway experiments showed that the solution offers a working routing protocol with a maximum routing time of 3 bytes. Though quick enough, end devices located farthest from the port of the server link will be bidirectionally supported. © 2022 Author(s).
