AB  - The Internet of Things (IoT) ecosystem continues to expand rapidly across domains such as smart agriculture, environmental monitoring, and urban infrastructure. Despite its potential, conventional cloud-centric IoT architectures remain constrained by scalability, latency, and resilience limitations, particularly in rural or resource-constrained regions. This paper presents NeuroMesh, a decentralized edge mesh framework built upon ESP32 devices, incorporating optional LoRa overlays and a planned autonomous control layer. NeuroMesh leverages a WiFi mesh backbone for low-latency multi-hop communication and extends coverage with LoRa-based long-range links. Experimental evaluation using a 6-node laboratory deployment demonstrates high packet delivery ratios (PDR), predictable latency growth with hop count, and energy efficiency under duty-cycled operation. Furthermore, a roadmap is provided for integrating Multi-Agent Reinforcement Learning (MARL), enabling adaptive routing, load balancing, and energy-aware decisions at the edge. NeuroMesh thus offers a resilient and extensible foundation for next-generation distributed IoT networks. © 2025 IEEE.
