AB  - This paper presents an experimental investigation of the practical communication range of LoRa, a widely available long-range radio technology operating at 923 MHz, commonly used in IoT applications. LoRa typically uses a star topology, where the range is limited by the single-hop distance between a node and a gateway. To overcome this limitation, mesh networking solutions such as Meshtastic can extend coverage by relaying messages through intermediate nodes. The experiment was conducted on a university campus, representing a dense and realistic deployment environment. Antenna heights were set approximately at human level rather than mounted on towers, to reflect typical mobile or handheld usage scenarios. A range of LoRa parameters was tested to evaluate their impact on communication distance. Results indicate that ground-level, point-to-point LoRa communication with legally limited transmit power achieves a usable range of approximately 700 m. The range can be significantly extended using a mesh network configuration. © 2025 IEEE.
