AD  - State Grid Anhui Electric Power Co., Ltd., Wuhu Power Supply Company, Wuhu, China
AB  - With the advancement of smart grids, power equipment status monitoring requires communication technologies that offer low power consumption, high reliability, and flexible deployment. Traditional RS485 wired communication is complex to install and difficult to scale [1], while existing wireless solutions such as Wi-Fi and 4G face limitations including high power consumption, severe signal attenuation, and dependence on public networks [2]. This paper proposes a low-power self-organizing communication method and terminal device based on LoRa, focusing on three innovative mechanisms: (1) Hybrid Duty-Cycle Control (HDCC), which combines scheduled and event-triggered wakeups for ultra-low power operation; (2) Self-Healing Multi-hop Routing (SHMR), which enables rapid switching and automatic topology repair when parent nodes fail; (3) Adaptive Tiered Retransmission (ATR), which enhances communication reliability using ACK confirmation and graded delay retransmissions. Field experiments conducted in a 500 kV substation with strong electromagnetic interference demonstrated an average packet loss rate of only 0.08%, terminal endurance of 6-12 months, and network coverage over 5 km, significantly outperforming Wi-Fi, 4G, and ZigBee. The proposed solution is applicable not only to power equipment monitoring but also to smart agriculture, industrial IoT, and similar scenarios. © 2025 IEEE.
