AD  - Hanoi University of Science and Technology (HUST), Hanoi, Viet Nam
AB  - In response to the growing concern over air pollution in urban areas, this paper presents the design and validation of a mobile air quality monitoring system. The system integrates multiple environmental sensors to measure PM1.0, PM2.5, PM10, CO, NO2, SO2, and O3, along with temperature and humidity. Each mobile station is built on ESP32 microcontrollers and communicates via Long Range (LoRa) modules; multi-hop routing is enabled by the Ad-hoc On-Demand Distance Vector (AODV) protocol. Data are aggregated at a gateway and uploaded to a cloud-based Message Queuing Telemetry Transport (MQTT) broker, with real-time visualization using Node-RED. Unlike prior implementations that rely on fixed gateways or single-hop links, this study contributes a reproducible framework and a real-world validation of AODV-enabled LoRa for mobile urban sensing. Experiments with multiple mobile nodes in city environments demonstrate reliable communication (95% message success rate, ≈120 ms latency), accurate pollutant measurements, and seamless cloud integration. The paper offers methodological insights into routing robustness and energy-aware design, bridging experimental networking research and deployable environmental monitoring.  © 2025 IEEE.
