AB  - The Internet of Things (IoT) increasingly relies on LoRa (Long Range) technology for low-power, wide-area communication. While LoRa typically uses a star topology, this structure limits coverage and increases network overhead. This research explores an alternative LoRa mesh topology using the AODV (Ad Hoc On-Demand Distance Vector) routing protocol, aiming to enhance network efficiency for environmental monitoring applications. A prototype consisting of five Arduino UNO nodes with Cytron LoRa-RFM Shields was developed and tested in both Line-of-Sight (LoS) and Non-Line-of-Sight (NLoS) conditions. Results showed a 1 km coverage in LoS scenarios, with 13.32% higher throughput and a 66.4% reduction in end-to-end delay compared to NLoS environments. These improvements are attributed to reduced signal attenuation and interference. Field test results confirm that the AODV protocol significantly improves data transmission efficiency and packet delivery in LoRa-based mesh networks. The findings offer practical insights for optimizing LoRa deployments in real-world IoT applications. © 2025 IEEE.
