AB  - Reliable communication in disaster scenarios is often disrupted when conventional cellular or satellite infrastructure fails. This work proposes a satellite-free, mesh-driven LoRa network model that enables resilient long-range connectivity through duty-cycle-aware multi-hop routing. A MATLAB-based simulation framework was developed to analyze the performance of the proposed model under post-disaster conditions. Key metrics such as Packet Delivery Ratio (PDR), latency, energy consumption, and coverage were evaluated and compared against single-hop LoRa and basic mesh topologies. Results show that the proposed duty-cycle-aware mesh architecture achieves up to 25% improvement in PDR, 30% lower latency, and enhanced fault tolerance under node failures. The study demonstrates that integrating duty-cycle optimization with mesh-driven routing can provide scalable, energy-efficient, and regulation-compliant communication in disaster zones without satellite dependency. © 2025 IEEE.
