AB  - Disasters and emergencies frequently disrupt conventional communication infrastructure, critically impeding coordination and response efforts during these events. This study presents ATA-AIKON, an integrated mesh network platform based on LoRaWAN, which is designed to provide uninterrupted communication under disaster conditions. The system combines five key components within a single platform: a custom mesh routing protocol, a novel encryption algorithm based on the Collatz conjecture, integration of four environmental sensors (temperature, humidity, air quality, and radiation) for CBRN early warning, solar-powered autonomous operation with adaptive power staging, and comprehensive disaster simulation testing. Eight identical nodes were deployed across the Atatürk University campus and evaluated over a 180-day field-testing period. The results indicate an average packet delivery ratio of 92.4% ± 1.31 across the network, with 94.2% connectivity maintained even under two-node failure conditions. The Collatz-based encryption algorithm consumed 63% less RAM than AES-128 on embedded systems (64 bytes vs. 176 bytes), with a throughput of 880 KB/s—well above the maximum LoRa data rate. The change-of-value reporting strategy reduced data traffic by an estimated 60–70%, and the weighted average power consumption of 38.2 mW enabled continuous off-grid operation. A five-year total cost of ownership analysis (USD 22,900) demonstrated substantial cost advantages over commercial Terrestrial Trunked Radio (TETRA) and satellite systems, while achieving higher reliability (97.2%). These findings suggest that ATA-AIKON represents a viable, cost-effective, and resilient solution for disaster communication; however, further validation across diverse geographies and independent cryptanalysis of the proposed encryption algorithm are recommended. © 2026 The Author.
