AD  - Science, Technology and Innovation Unit, The Abdus Salam International Centre for Theoretical Physics (ICTP), Trieste, Italy
AD  - Department of Information and Communications Engineering, College of Information Engineering, Al-Nahrain University, Baghdad, Iraq
AB  - Low-power wide-area networks based on LoRa mesh networking offer a promising communication solution for infrastructure-limited indoor environments, where direct links are frequently obstructed by walls and multiple floors. However, experimentally validated guidance on relay deployment, traffic configuration and per-role energy consumption for Meshtastic-based mesh networks under multi-floor non-line-of-sight (NLOS) conditions remains limited. To address this gap, the communication and energy performance of a Meshtastic LoRa mesh network were experimentally evaluated in a four-storey indoor NLOS environment, considering relay availability, relay placement, traffic load and network role. A six-node testbed of heterogeneous LilyGO devices running Meshtastic firmware 2.7 exchanged acknowledged direct messages in the EU 868 MHz band, with communication performance quantified by packet delivery ratio (PDR), received signal strength and signal-to-noise ratio, and per-role energy demand measured using an Otii arc power analyser. Introducing a single relay increased the PDR from 1.0% for direct communication to 96.5%. When two relays were available, forwarding was unevenly distributed between them (66.5% versus 33.5%) in favour of the relay with the stronger link quality. Relay placement dominated end-to-end reliability, in which a relay deployed on an intermediate floor (L0) achieved a PDR of 99.25%, compared with 38.50% for direct communication and 12.00% when the relay was colocated with the receiver (L2) and shared its bottleneck link. Reducing the offered traffic load improved per-node PDR from 0.6%–58.3% under fully active transmission to 20.5–85.7% under mixed traffic. Energy measurements showed near-identical demand across the transmitter, receiver and relay roles (125–126 mA average current, 467–470 mJ per message and an estimated 25–26 h lifetime on a 3200 mAh battery), indicating that relaying imposes no measurable energy penalty under always-on operation. These findings provide experimentally validated deployment guidelines, demonstrating that communication reliability in multi-floor indoor environments is governed by relay placement that partitions the dominant obstruction and by traffic configuration, and can be improved without additional per-node energy cost across the evaluated active roles. © 2026 The Author(s). IET Communications published by John Wiley & Sons Ltd on behalf of The Institution of Engineering and Technology.
