AD  - Amrita School of Computing, Amrita Vishwa Vidyapeetham, Nagercoil, India
AB  - With the growing popularity of Internet of Things (IoT) applications, effective and dependable communication protocols are needed for low-power wide-area networks (LPWANs) including long-range (LoRa) WAN. There are several methods designed for optimal routing in LoRa; still, several challenges like instability, inaccurate decision-making, and limited longevity limit efficient communication. In order to overcome the challenges, a novel approach integrating improved kookaburra optimization (IKO) for cluster head (CH) selection and deep reinforcement learning (DRL) for multi-hop routing in LoRa networks is proposed. IKO enhances the selection process of CHs by optimizing cluster formation, considering factors such as remaining energy, distance, and delay. DRL-based multi-hop routing is employed for routing the information from the CH to the gateway. The proposed approach aims to improve routing efficiency, energy consumption, and network lifetime in LoRa networks, thus facilitating the deployment of IoT applications in diverse domains. Experimental results demonstrate the effectiveness of the proposed IKO-based CH selection and DRL-based multi-hop routing in enhancing the performance of LoRa networks compared to traditional approaches. The analysis of the proposed IKO + DRL in terms of throughput, delay, packet delivery ratio, energy consumption, and network lifetime acquired the values of 148.167, 1.8, 98.849, 13.437, and 94.713, respectively. Both IKO-based clustering and DRL-based multi-hop routing contribute to the scalability and flexibility of LoRa networks. © The Author(s), under exclusive licence to Società Italiana di Fisica and Springer-Verlag GmbH Germany, part of Springer Nature 2026.
