AD  - VIT-AP University, School Of Computer Science And Engineering (SCOPE), Andhra Pradesh, Amaravati, 522241, India
AB  - LoRaWAN provides long-range and low-power connectivity for Internet of Things (IoT) deployments, but mobile and sparse deployments may suffer from coverage holes, weak direct gateway reachability, and downlink ACK limitations. Existing relay-assisted LoRa approaches mainly focus on packet delivery, delay, or energy efficiency, while the preservation of multi-gateway observations after relay forwarding remains underexplored. This paper proposes Gateway-Diversity-Preserving Multi-Hop Relay (GDP-MH), a LoRaWAN-oriented relay overlay that selects relays not only for delivery recovery but also for preserving gateway observation richness at the Network Server. A Gateway Diversity Estimate (GDE) is introduced to capture each relay's ability to produce multi-gateway packet observations. The framework is further extended through TI-GDP-MH, a time-aware downlink ACK gateway-selection extension that keeps the GDP-MH uplink relay path unchanged and applies ACKScore-FGCC-based gateway selection for confirmed traffic. A six-mode relay evaluation is conducted against single-hop LoRaWAN, sink-oriented multi-hop, opportunistic RSSI, distance-layer relay selection, and a GDE-disabled ablation baseline across four mobility and gateway-density scenarios using an ADR-like SF-aware link model. Results show that GDP-MH preserves relay-level uplink delivery, remaining within approximately 0.23 percentage points of the strongest non-GDP delivery-focused relay baseline. Across the four scenarios, GDP-MH improves the mean gateway-observation count by 4.13-28.74%, TOR2 by 5.23-54.19%, and TOR3 by 7.75-87.66% over the strongest non-GDP delivery-focused relay baseline. Additional fixed-SF and dense-device tests confirm the robustness of the gateway-diversity trend. TI-GDP-MH is evaluated separately as a feasibility-gated downlink ACK-selection extension, and the ACK-time position-error analysis shows that time-aware ACK selection can support downlink decisions when multiple feasible gateways and position uncertainty are present.  © 2013 IEEE.
